. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2 *  
. 
.         * We run a logistic mixed effects model with random intercepts at the
>  individual (pid) and household (cid) levels.
.         * We predict the party preference for parties on the left of the Germ
> an spectrum (used to operationalize left-leaning 
.         * political preferences) from whether the person has daughters (1 = y
> es, 0 = everything else), controlling for a year-
.         * fixed effect.
. 
.                 
.         * xtmelogit left_leaning hasdaughters  syear || cid: || pid: , intpoi
> nts(3)
.         
.         * Because the model is not fully computationally reproducible, we cha
> nge the estimation approach. We instead use a logistic
.         * panel regression with random effects at the level of participants (
> pid). 
.         * We predict the party preference for parties on the left of the Germ
> an spectrum (used to operationalize left-leaning 
.         * political preferences) from whether the person has daughters (1 = y
> es, 0 = everything else), controlling for a year-
.         * fixed effect.
. 
